1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,845
Who is calling our API, and how do we know?

2
00:00:02,845 --> 00:00:07,952
Until now, an admin was just a name in a header, and anyone can type a header.

3
00:00:07,952 --> 00:00:14,047
Keycloak now holds every login: the bank's admins, the merchants, and the merchants' servers.

4
00:00:14,047 --> 00:00:20,238
The realm defines two roles, admin and partner, and a client the backend uses for itself.

5
00:00:20,238 --> 00:00:26,369
Signing up now takes a password, which goes straight to Keycloak and never into our database.

6
00:00:26,369 --> 00:00:31,523
The new login can do nothing until the email is verified, and then it gets the partner role.

7
00:00:31,523 --> 00:00:37,488
Every request now carries a token, and the app checks it before any business code runs.

8
00:00:37,488 --> 00:00:43,738
Admin routes need the admin role, partner routes need the partner role, and anything not listed is refused.

9
00:00:43,738 --> 00:00:48,476
The admin's name now comes from the token, so the history shows who really acted.

10
00:00:48,476 --> 00:00:54,892
An active merchant creates API clients for its servers, and each secret is shown only once.

11
00:00:54,892 --> 00:00:59,869
Rotating issues a new secret, and the old one stops working at once.

12
00:00:59,869 --> 00:01:06,678
One integration test runs the whole onboarding on a real Postgres, Redis, Mailpit, and Keycloak.

13
00:01:06,678 --> 00:01:12,738
Under load, every scenario passed with real tokens, from sign-ups to secret rotation.

14
00:01:12,738 --> 00:01:15,904
Next, the websites that will actually take the payments.

